'''Meiji 3 Intercalary October 17, Dajōkan Edict No. 745''' was a {{ill|Dajōkan Edict|ja|太政官布告・太政官達}} issued on {{ill|December 9, 1870|ja|1870年12月9日}} (Meiji 3 Intercalary October 17). It is also known as the '''Prohibition of Tensha Shinto''' (天社禁止令) or the '''Prohibition of Tensha Shinto Practices''' (天社神道禁止令).

This edict was issued as part of the Meiji government’s policy of abolishing the {{ill|Onmyōryō|ja|陰陽寮}}. Under this proclamation, {{ill|Onmyōji|ja|陰陽師}}—recognized as an official profession—were officially banned from continuing their practice.

== Contents ==
{{Quotation|In the past, individuals calling themselves practitioners of {{ill|Tensha Shinto|ja|天社土御門神道}} and receiving licenses from the {{ill|Tsuchimikado family|ja|土御門家（安倍氏）}} have been found wearing swords and carrying {{ill|Emblems|ja|会符}} as they pass through {{ill|stations|ja|宿場}}. This practice is entirely unfounded, and henceforth these actions are prohibited and must be strictly enforced. Furthermore, the granting of any further licenses to disciples is entirely forbidden, as was recently ordered to {{ill|Tsuchimikado Haruaki|ja|土御門晴榮}}. All {{ill|Prefectures|ja|府藩県三治制}} must observe this decree and ensure the control of their jurisdictions accordingly.<ref>{{NDLDC|787950/259|Law Compilation, Meiji 3|format=EXTERNAL}}, Section 745, accessed August 4, 2017.</ref>|{{ill|Dajōkan|ja|太政官 (明治時代)}}}}
{{Quote|(Modern Translation) In the past, individuals identifying themselves as practitioners of {{ill|Tensha Shinto|ja|天社土御門神道}} and licensed by the {{ill|Tsuchimikado family|ja|土御門家}}, have been observed wearing swords and displaying {{ill|Emblems|ja|会符}} at {{ill|post stations|ja|宿駅}}. This is deemed entirely unjustified. Henceforth, these activities are strictly prohibited. Furthermore, all licensing of disciples is entirely banned, as conveyed to {{ill|Tsuchimikado Haruaki|ja|土御門晴榮}}. All {{ill|Prefectures|ja|府藩県}} must adhere to this directive and ensure appropriate oversight within their jurisdictions.|{{ill|Dajōkan|ja|太政官 (明治時代)}}}}

